**Read:** [Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe](../sources/Thermodynamics-and-Chemistry-DeVoe.lingua#r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1), second-law foundations; [Engineering Thermodynamics — Olivier Cleynen](../sources/Engineering-Thermodynamics-Cleynen.lingua#r_38CM7HKS0GSN5T09YDXFKHRVGQ), irreversibility and the second law; [Advanced Thermodynamics — MIT 2.43, Spring 2024](../sources/Advanced-Thermodynamics-MIT-2-43.lingua#r_5GXQQ35FEFJM34SWA12X8QHGKC), precise thermodynamic definitions.

A reversible idealization permits the system and surroundings to be restored with no other net change. It is a limit against which actual processes can be compared, not an instruction that every slow machine achieves that limit. Friction, mixing, reaction away from equilibrium, electrical resistance and heat transfer across a finite temperature difference generate entropy.

A thermal reservoir maintains a specified temperature while exchanging the relevant heat, as an approximation over the studied duty. A finite body generally changes temperature; treating it as an infinite reservoir can overstate available work or misstate an entropy balance.

Construct a process that is quasistatic but dissipative, and another that is adiabatic but irreversible. Explain each distinction using a full system/surroundings account. Later microscopic reversibility and fluctuation statements require additional definitions and do not erase these macroscopic accounting conditions.

